Signals the C library gets wrong, from libc-test. raise signals the
calling thread, as C and POSIX say, not the process, where another
thread could take the signal and raise could return before its
handler ran; signals stay blocked while it finds the thread and sends,
as in musl, so a handler that forks cannot leave the child signalling
its parent (regression/raise-race). The signals from 32 up to SIGRTMIN
are the C library's own (SIGCANCEL, SIGTIMER): sigaddset fails them
with EINVAL, sigfillset leaves them out, and sigprocmask and
pthread_sigmask never show them in the mask they give back, as in
glibc and musl (regression/sigprocmask-internal); the library itself
adds them to a set with mlibc::sigaddset_internal (mlibc/sigset.hpp),
as pthread_setcancelstate does for SIGCANCEL. Meant for upstream.

